
# 24/7 Infrastructure Monitoring & Alerting on AWS

A real-time monitoring and alerting solution built on **Amazon CloudWatch** and **Amazon SNS**, designed to track the health of EC2 instances (and extendable to RDS/VPC) and notify the team immediately when something breaks.

![Status](https://img.shields.io/badge/status-complete-brightgreen)
![AWS](https://img.shields.io/badge/AWS-CloudWatch%20%7C%20SNS%20%7C%20EC2-orange)

---

## 📌 Project Overview

| | |
|---|---|
| **Goal** | Continuous (24/7) visibility into infrastructure health, with automated alerting on threshold breaches |
| **Core Services** | Amazon EC2, CloudWatch Agent, CloudWatch Metrics & Alarms, CloudWatch Dashboards, Amazon SNS |
| **Monitored Metrics** | CPU Utilization, Memory Usage, Disk Usage, Network In/Out |
| **Alert Channel** | Email (via SNS subscription) |
| **Skill Level** | Beginner–Intermediate AWS Cloud Operations |

---

## 🏗️ Architecture

```
EC2 Instance
     │
     ▼  (CPU, network, disk-I/O — native metrics)
     ▼  (memory, disk-space — via CloudWatch Agent)
CloudWatch Metrics
     │
     ├──► CloudWatch Dashboards   (real-time visualization)
     │
     └──► CloudWatch Alarms       (threshold breach detection)
                │
                ▼
          Amazon SNS Topic
                │
                ▼
       Email Subscriber(s)
```

> **Note:** EC2 only reports CPU, network, and disk I/O metrics natively. Memory and disk-space usage require the **CloudWatch Agent** to be installed and configured on the instance — this is the first major step below.

---

## 🛠️ Step-by-Step Build Process

### 1. Launch and Prepare the EC2 Instance
- Launched an EC2 instance (Amazon Linux 2023).
- Attached an IAM role with the `CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy` permission so the instance can push custom metrics to CloudWatch.

### 2. Install the CloudWatch Agent
```bash
sudo yum install amazon-cloudwatch-agent -y
```



### 3. Run the CloudWatch Agent Configuration Wizard
```bash
sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-config-wizard
```
Configured the wizard to collect:
- CPU, memory, and disk metrics





### 4. Fetch the Config and Start the Agent
```bash
sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl \
  -a fetch-config -m ec2 -s \
  -c file:/opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/etc/amazon-cloudwatch-agent.toml
```
```bash
sudo systemctl restart amazon-cloudwatch-agent
sudo systemctl status amazon-cloudwatch-agent
```



### 5. Build a CloudWatch Dashboard
- Navigated to **CloudWatch Console → Dashboards → Create dashboard**
- Added widgets for: `CPUUtilization`, `mem_used_percent`, `disk_used_percent`, `NetworkIn`/`NetworkOut`



### 6. Create a CloudWatch Alarm
- Metric: `CPUUtilization`
- Statistic: Average | Period: 5 minutes
- Threshold: Greater than 80% for 3 consecutive datapoints (avoids false positives from brief spikes)



### 7. Set Up the SNS Topic & Email Subscription
```bash
# Done via console:
# SNS → Topics → Create topic → "infra-alerts" (Standard type)
# SNS → Create subscription → Protocol: Email → confirmed via inbox link
```



### 8. Connect the Alarm to SNS
- Alarm action on **"In alarm"** state → publish to `infra-alerts` SNS topic

### 9. Test and Validate
- Simulated CPU load using `stress` to trigger the alarm
- Confirmed email notification was received within ~5 minutes





## ✅ Outcome

- Continuous visibility into EC2 health via CloudWatch Dashboards
- Automated email alerts on sustained CPU threshold breaches, avoiding noisy single-spike false alarms
- Extendable architecture — RDS and VPC metrics can be added the same way






## 🧠 Key Concepts Demonstrated
- CloudWatch Agent installation & custom metric collection (memory/disk beyond EC2 defaults)
- CloudWatch Alarms with multi-datapoint thresholds to reduce alert fatigue
- SNS topic/subscription model for notification fan-out
- IAM least-privilege role scoping for the CloudWatch Agent
- Real-world debugging of config file paths, typos, and missing dependencies

